Hold the Fort — October 1

A major defense-tech signal crossed the threshold overnight: the Pentagon announced a new Autonomous Warfare Command — “AUTOWARCOM” on September 30. It is intended to become a four-star-level organization focused on rapidly developing, buying and fielding drones, robotics and AI-enabled autonomous systems across the services. 

This materially raises my confidence in the career/contracting thesis we’ve been building. Autonomy is moving from scattered programs toward institutionalized demand with its own command structure. For your career search, I would now weight roles touching autonomous systems, mission engineering, rapid acquisition, product management and operator↔engineer translation more heavily than generic product-management roles. For a future SDVOSB, AUTOWARCOM’s eventual acquisition ecosystem becomes a target surface worth mapping.

There’s an immediate industrial clue too: Ukraine is scrambling to counter faster jet-powered attack drones, and a reported bottleneck is miniature turbojet availability, alongside AI guidance, cooling and interceptor production.  That’s exactly the kind of second-order constraint Pantheon/Goblin should notice: the strategic system may be “autonomous warfare,” while the economically scarce object can be an obscure component several layers underneath it.

Goblin — our WAIT call gets stronger, not weaker

The physical-AI thesis keeps receiving evidence, but today’s macro regime is hostile to chasing it. Reuters reports the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield around 5.3%, after touching 5.34%, amid the largest quarterly global bond-yield rise this century; Brent is again around $100. Strong Micron results are supporting AI hardware, but high yields are simultaneously compressing the valuation environment. 

AMD is around its highs after gaining almost 30% in September and roughly 200% YTD, with the World Labs acquisition reinforcing its move from chips into spatial/physical AI. 

Paper action: WAIT on AMD. No chase.

The more interesting confirmation is memory: Micron’s results are another observation that the AI bottleneck is propagating outward from accelerators into memory, networking, power and complete systems. HPE has also disclosed a $1.2B AMD Helios rack deal with Vultr for AI training and inference infrastructure. 

Our search remains:

AI demand → identify necessary layer → measure supply elasticity → find the slowest-expanding constraint → buy before narrative catches it

rather than “AI good → buy AI stock.”

Surveillance — federal legislation is now real, not hypothetical

Yesterday’s threshold crossing is confirmed today: Josh Hawley introduced legislation targeting abuse of Flock and similar AI-powered ALPR systems. Reporting describes proposed warrant requirements for certain searches, a 30-day retention rule absent legal authorization, auditing requirements, restrictions on sale to data brokers and penalties for misuse. 

And today’s real-world evidence illustrates why provenance matters: officials in St. Lucie County, Florida reportedly discovered 14 unpermitted Flock cameras, with 11 initially unclaimed by local law enforcement. 

That sharpens yesterday’s prediction. The emerging regulatory object isn’t merely privacy; it is authority provenance:

Who installed sensor?
→ under what authority?
→ who can query it?
→ for what purpose?
→ how long is data retained?
→ who can receive it?
→ is every action auditable?

That architecture is likely to matter far beyond ALPRs.

